//! Entries slice 96 — Batch 240 (Plan 242 / Research 271): the P67–P70
//! conversion — three own `kernel_opt` rules from the 2026-10-15 llama.cpp
//! envelope window (`e467e5b5e5ba` → `e36df604`, staged by the watch poll
//! at commit `22fe7777c`).
//!
//! - P67 `9166ceaf` #30421 "opencl: fix OOB tail access for adreno
//!   noshuffle gemv kernels" (MIT) — the subgroup TAIL-LANE law, category
//!   E. 13 kernels, 225 clamped read sites; every quote verbatim at the
//!   fetched `.patch` this session.
//! - P68 `23f83e05` #30526 "ggml-cpu : optimize `pad_f32`" (MIT) — the pad
//!   ROW-CLASSIFICATION law, category A. The old 4-deep i0-i3 scalar nest
//!   carried `// TODO: optimize`; every quote verbatim at the `.patch`.
//! - P69 `5a638aed` #29200 "cpu: add F16 `SWIGLU_OAI` reference
//!   implementation and validation" (MIT) — the f16-ACTIVATION-UPCAST-
//!   AT-FOREIGN-GRAPH-TRANSLATION law, category B; the commit cites its
//!   own in-tree precedent (`translate_glu_swiglu_clamp`) — the
//!   pattern-law class.
//! - P70 `733f57a9` #27703 "vulkan : copy strided f16 KV once on AMD so
//!   reads spread across memory channels" (MIT) — graded B/watch, NOT
//!   converted: the memory-channel-spread law is vendor-specific (AMD
//!   channel aliasing, gated AMD-not-RDNA4) and no Rust-side host greps
//!   up in our trees (our KV reads are tiled/coalesced by design — the
//!   A-category family owns that axis). Disposition in Research 271.

use super::KernelRule;

pub fn entries() -> Vec<KernelRule> {
    vec![
        // ── E: the subgroup tail-lane law — every lane of a collective
        // stays LIVE; its loads CLAMP to a valid row; only its stores
        // gate on the real bound. ─
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "subgroup-tail-lane-clamp-live-loads-gate-stores".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "MECHANISM (subgroup tail-lane safety; category E — parallelism/lane-participation contract): a SUBGROUP operation (subgroup broadcast/reduce, warp shuffle, simdgroup op) is COLLECTIVE — every lane of the group executes it in lockstep, so a work mapping that lays row-pairs across lanes (`gid` indexes the row pair; `LINE_STRIDE_A = M/2`) cannot let the tail lanes (when the row count does not divide the group's span) early-exit: a lane that branches away from a collective op is undefined behavior on subgroup vendors (Adreno/OpenCL hangs). THE LAW, verbatim in-source (llama.cpp #30421 @ 9166ceaf): '// Keep tail lanes active for subgroup ops, but read a valid weight row.' THE LOAD HALF — clamp, do not skip: `uint gid_s = min(gid, LINE_STRIDE_A - 1);` and EVERY weight/scale read site uses the clamped index (`regS = src0_d[gid_s + k * LINE_STRIDE_A]; // each fiber loads scale of two rows`, and every `read_imageui(src0_q, (gid_s + k * BLOCK_STRIDE_A + LINE_STRIDE_A * n)).x`) — a DUPLICATE READ of the last valid row, safe because the tail lane's result is discarded by the store gate; the clamp must reach EVERY read site (225 `gid_s` sites across the 13 noshuffle gemv kernels of the commit — q1_0/q4_0/q4_0_spec/q4_1/q4_k+o4/q5_0/q5_1/q5_k/q6_k+o4/q8_0/iq4_nl, the mc3 and splitk variants) — ONE un-clamped read is the OOB. THE STORE HALF — gate, do not clamp: the whole store block wraps in the row bound (`if (gid < LINE_STRIDE_A) { vstore2((float2)(acc.s0, acc.s1), 0, &(dst[0 * M + gid * 2])); ... }`) — a clamped STORE would write a duplicate into the last valid row (a race), so the store takes the TRUE bound; and on the pair/splitk variants the vector store SPLITS into per-element scalar guards, verbatim: '// Keep tail stores inside this K-slice.' + `if (gid * 2 + 0 < M) partial[kslice * M + gid * 2 + 0] = totalSum.s0;` + `if (gid * 2 + 1 < M) partial[kslice * M + gid * 2 + 1] = totalSum.s1;` (the two-row vstore2 becomes two independently-guarded scalar stores — the bound falls BETWEEN the pair elements, so the vector store cannot be guarded as one). THE ASYMMETRY IS THE LAW: loads clamp (any valid index satisfies the collective — the value is garbage but unused), stores gate (the real bound — a clamped store corrupts a valid row); a gated load is IMPOSSIBLE (the collective op needs a value on every lane). REGIME: subgroup/warp/simdgroup collectives over a row domain that does not divide the group span — our simd paths (warp shuffles in the GDN scan reductions, Metal simdgroup ops, WGSL subgroups) own the same contract: the reduction needs all lanes too. ANTI-REGIME: none — this is the correctness posture of every collective; the alternative shape (pad the grid so no tail exists, the ragged-chunk-pad STRATEGY) pays memory for uniformity and owns a different axis. KINSHIP (do not double-count): tail-clamped-row-reuse-widens-fast-kernel-admission (B205) is the SAME clamp+suppress MECHANISM on the ADMISSION-WIDENING face — it clamps tail rows so an any-N fast path exists and the duplicate work's stores are suppressed because the REAL rows' outputs are all kept; THIS owns the PARTICIPATION-SAFETY face — the tail lanes produce NOTHING, they stay alive only because the collective requires them, so the clamp exists for memory safety alone; masked-boundary-load (popcorn) is the per-element LOAD GUARD (skips the OOB load of a masked kernel) — a lane feeding a collective CANNOT skip the load, it must redirect it; role-scoped-store-must-match-compute-guard (entries_gpu_29) aligns store predicates WITH compute predicates under role partitioning — THIS deliberately DECOUPLES them (compute runs wider than store: every lane computes, only in-bound lanes store); warp-shuffle-intrinsics-for-sub-warp-reduction (entries_gpu_01) owns the reduction MECHANISM this rule keeps live. NotBenchable here (an OOB correctness repair on OpenCL/Adreno; no perf number ships in the patch — our simd/warp lanes adopt the contract, not a measured gain). Source: ggml-org/llama.cpp @ 9166ceaf16599406366cd2d01dc6a2126bbada4d (#30421, MIT) — the 13 kernel files (the in-source law comment + the gid_s clamp + the store guards + the splitk scalar pair), every quote verbatim at the fetched .patch this session. Batch 240 / Plan 242 / Research 271.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// naive: the tail lane either reads OOB at its raw row index or\n// early-returns -- but the subgroup broadcast inside the accumulate\n// is COLLECTIVE: a lane that branched away from it is UB on subgroup\n// vendors, and the raw-index reads are OOB past LINE_STRIDE_A.\nfn gemv_lane_naive(\n    gid: usize,            // this lane's row-pair index\n    line_stride_a: usize,  // M / 2 row pairs\n    m: usize,              // real row count\n    scales: &[u32],\n    weights: &[u32],\n    partial: &mut [f32],\n) {\n    if gid >= line_stride_a {\n        return; // WRONG: the collective below needs EVERY lane live\n    }\n    let scale = scales[gid];               // raw index (ok only via the return)\n    let w0 = weights[gid];                 // ...and every other read too\n    let acc = dequant_accum(w0, scale);    // contains subgroup_broadcast -> UB\n    partial[gid * 2] = acc.0;              // tail rows were 'handled' by the return\n    partial[gid * 2 + 1] = acc.1;\n}\n\nfn dequant_accum(_w: u32, _s: u32) -> (f32, f32) {\n    // subgroup_broadcast lives here: every lane of the group must arrive\n    (0.0, 0.0)\n}".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// optimized: the tail lane stays LIVE (the collective needs it), its\n// loads CLAMP to a valid row (gid_s = min(gid, LINE_STRIDE_A - 1)), and\n// only its STORES gate on the real bound -- the pair store splits into\n// per-element guards because the bound can fall BETWEEN the two rows.\nfn gemv_lane_tail_safe(\n    gid: usize,\n    line_stride_a: usize,\n    m: usize,\n    scales: &[u32],\n    weights: &[u32],\n    partial: &mut [f32],\n) {\n    // \"Keep tail lanes active for subgroup ops, but read a valid weight row.\"\n    let gid_s = gid.min(line_stride_a - 1); // duplicate of the last valid row\n    let scale = scales[gid_s];              // EVERY read site uses the clamp\n    let w0 = weights[gid_s];\n    let acc = dequant_accum(w0, scale);     // collective: every lane participates\n    // \"Keep tail stores inside this K-slice.\"\n    if gid * 2 + 0 < m {\n        partial[gid * 2 + 0] = acc.0;       // per-element bound guards\n    }\n    if gid * 2 + 1 < m {\n        partial[gid * 2 + 1] = acc.1;       // (the vector pair store splits)\n    }\n}\n\nfn dequant_accum(_w: u32, _s: u32) -> (f32, f32) {\n    // subgroup_broadcast lives here: every lane of the group must arrive\n    (0.0, 0.0)\n}".to_string(),
        },
        // ── A: the pad ROW-CLASSIFICATION law — the pad op executes as
        // row classes: interior+contig rows are memset+memcpy+memset,
        // boundary rows are one whole-row memset, threading runs on the
        // flattened row axis. ─
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "pad-row-classification-interior-memcpy".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "MECHANISM (pad-op execution structure; category A — memory access/data movement): a pad op's cost is classified at the ROW, never the element — the old ggml-cpu pad_f32 was a 4-deep i0-i3 scalar nest re-testing all four pad-position predicates PER ELEMENT and carried a standing `// TODO: optimize`; the fix restructures into THREE row classes under ROW-MAJOR THREADING (`for (int64_t row = ith; row < nrows; row += nth)` over the flattened (ne1,ne2,ne3) destination row space — the per-row work is the parallel unit, not the i1 axis nested inside i2/i3). CLASS (1) INTERIOR — every outer-dim pad position inside the source bounds (`const bool interior = (i1 >= lp1 && i1 < ne1 - rp1) && (i2 >= lp2 && i2 < ne2 - rp2) && (i3 >= lp3 && i3 < ne3 - rp3);`) — the row is a SOURCE-ROW COPY: when the source row is contiguous (`const bool src_row_contig = src0->nb[0] == sizeof(float);`, hoisted once) and the row-inner pads are non-negative, the whole row materializes as THREE libc calls with zero per-element arithmetic: `memset(dst_row, 0, lp0 * sizeof(float)); memcpy(dst_row + lp0, src_row, ne00 * sizeof(float)); memset(dst_row + lp0 + ne00, 0, rp0 * sizeof(float));`. CLASS (2) the non-contig / negative-inner-pad interior row keeps the scalar per-element loop over i0 with the inner bound test (`dst_row[i0] = *(const float *)(src_row + (i0 - lp0)*nb00);` inside the window, `dst_row[i0] = 0.0f;` outside). CLASS (3) BOUNDARY — any outer dim inside a pad band — the whole row is zeros, ONE memset and continue: `memset(dst_row, 0, ne0 * sizeof(float)); continue;`. THE CIRCULAR VARIANT KEEPS ITS FULL-ROW WRAP-AROUND PATH (no classification — `if constexpr (circular_t)` walks `ggml_wrap_around` per element across the whole row; classification is the linear-pad contract). DECISION CONTENT: (a) the row test hoists 4-per-element predicates into 1-per-row; (b) the CONTIGUITY HOIST licenses memcpy — the byte copy is exact iff the source row is stride-1 (`nb[0] == sizeof(float)`), checked once, not per row; (c) the negative-pad carve-out (`lp0 >= 0 && rp0 >= 0`) keeps the memcpy window inside the row (negative pads CROP, not pad — the window math differs); (d) whole-row-zero rows (the outer-dim pad bands) are the cheapest class of all — one memset, no source touch. REGIME: CPU pad ops over tensors whose outer dims are mostly interior (the common case — pad bands are thin); the win scales with ne0 (row length) and the interior fraction. KINSHIP (do not double-count): ragged-chunk-pad (entries_popcorn) owns the pad-vs-bound STRATEGY axis (pad the input to block_size for uniform chunk processing + the B229 staging-symmetry rider) — THIS owns the pad OP's own execution once you have decided to pad; zero-pad-ragged-tail-additive-identity (B186) owns the additive ECONOMICS (zero pads as identity elements so the padded compute needs no special case) — THIS owns materializing the padded OUTPUT; pad-tail-to-fixed-tile (B84) pads the activation axis to fit a tile then STRIPS the synthetic rows — the WHY-to-pad, not the HOW; the B226 pad-at-the-producer rider (on fa-fast-path-validated-exact-shape-admission) owns WHERE the pad lives in the graph — placement, not execution; the P45 cached-zero-pad rung (the B195 pad ladder) owns where the ZERO BYTES come from (once per shape) — THIS assumes the destination write is owed regardless. NotBenchable here (no in-source number; the TODO:optimize → row-classified structure is the decision content; our CPU pad/tile-fill paths adopt the classification, not a measured row). Source: ggml-org/llama.cpp @ 23f83e056d88ddae0d56b01ccc65fa42ab6625f7 (#30526, MIT) — ggml-cpu/ops.cpp ggml_compute_forward_pad_f32 (the TODO:optimize it deletes, the row loop, src_row_contig, the interior predicate, the memset/memcpy/memset triple, the whole-row memset+continue, the circular carve-out), every quote verbatim at the fetched .patch this session. Batch 240 / Plan 242 / Research 271.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// naive: the 4-deep scalar nest — every element re-tests all four\n// pad-position predicates; threading rides i1 nested inside i2/i3.\nfn pad_naive(\n    dst: &mut [f32],\n    src: &[f32],\n    nb1: usize,\n    ne0: usize,\n    ne1: usize,\n    lp0: isize,\n    rp0: isize,\n    lp1: isize,\n    rp1: isize,\n    ne00: usize,\n    ith: usize,\n    nth: usize,\n) {\n    // TODO: optimize\n    for i2 in 0..1usize {\n        for i1 in (ith..ne1).step_by(nth) {\n            for i0 in 0..ne0 {\n                let idx = i1 * ne0 + i0;\n                let inside = i0 as isize >= lp0\n                    && (i0 as isize) < ne0 as isize - rp0\n                    && i1 as isize >= lp1\n                    && (i1 as isize) < ne1 as isize - rp1;\n                dst[idx] = if inside {\n                    let s0 = (i0 as isize - lp0) as usize;\n                    let s1 = (i1 as isize - lp1) as usize;\n                    src[s1 * nb1 + s0 * ne00.max(1)] // raw index math per element\n                } else {\n                    0.0\n                };\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// optimized: classify the ROW once — interior+contig rows are three\n// bulk calls, boundary rows are one whole-row zero, threading runs on\n// the flattened row axis.\nfn pad_row_classified(\n    dst: &mut [f32],\n    src: &[f32],\n    nb0: usize,\n    nb1: usize,\n    ne0: usize,\n    ne1: usize,\n    lp0: isize,\n    rp0: isize,\n    lp1: isize,\n    rp1: isize,\n    ne00: usize,\n    ith: usize,\n    nth: usize,\n) {\n    let nrows = ne1; // flattened outer-dim row count (ne1*ne2*ne3 in 4-D)\n    let src_row_contig = nb0 == 4; // src0->nb[0] == sizeof(float), hoisted once\n    for row in (ith..nrows).step_by(nth) {\n        let i1 = row;\n        let dst_row = &mut dst[row * ne0..(row + 1) * ne0];\n        let interior =\n            i1 as isize >= lp1 && (i1 as isize) < ne1 as isize - rp1;\n        if !interior {\n            dst_row.fill(0.0); // memset(dst_row, 0, ne0 * sizeof(float))\n            continue;\n        }\n        let src_row_off = ((i1 as isize - lp1) as usize) * nb1;\n        if src_row_contig && lp0 >= 0 && rp0 >= 0 {\n            let l = lp0 as usize;\n            let r = rp0 as usize;\n            dst_row[..l].fill(0.0); // memset(dst_row, 0, lp0)\n            dst_row[l..l + ne00].copy_from_slice(&src[src_row_off / 4..src_row_off / 4 + ne00]); // memcpy\n            dst_row[l + ne00..].fill(0.0); // memset(dst_row + lp0 + ne00, 0, rp0)\n        } else {\n            for (i0, d) in dst_row.iter_mut().enumerate() {\n                *d = if i0 as isize >= lp0 && (i0 as isize) < ne0 as isize - rp0 {\n                    let s0 = (i0 as isize - lp0) as usize;\n                    src[src_row_off / 4 + s0 * nb0 / 4]\n                } else {\n                    0.0\n                };\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}".to_string(),
        },
        // ── B: the f16-activation-upcast-at-foreign-graph-translation law
        // — at a foreign-graph boundary the constants pin the subgraph
        // dtype; upcast the INPUTS, build in f32, convert back at the
        // end; a backend without the kernel refuses; the reference
        // carries a tolerance that names its reason. ─
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "f16-upcast-at-foreign-graph-translation".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "MECHANISM (dtype at a foreign-graph translation boundary; category B — compute/precision upcast): when a graph is TRANSLATED for a foreign runtime (ggml-openvino building OpenVINO ops), the foreign graph's CONSTANT nodes pin the subgraph dtype — `alpha_const` and `one` are created f32 UNCONDITIONALLY (`ov::op::v0::Constant::create(ov::element::f32, ...)`) — and the foreign runtime's typed elementwise ops require matching input types, so a narrower activation (f16 src) dies at the boundary with the foreign runtime's own words: 'Swish inputs must have the same type but they are: f16 and f32'. THE FIX PATTERN — and the commit CITES ITS OWN IN-TREE PRECEDENT for it, verbatim: 'translate_glu_swiglu_clamp already upcasts to f32 and converts back at the end; apply the same pattern here.' (the second instance of a pattern the source already carried = the pattern-law class: the FIRST fix proved the shape, the second makes it the rule). THE SHAPE: upcast every non-f32 INPUT at the graph boundary (`if (src0.get_element_type() != ov::element::f32) { src0 = std::make_shared<ov::op::v0::Convert>(src0, ov::element::f32); }` and the src1 twin), build the foreign subgraph entirely in f32, and convert the RESULT back to the CALLER's output type at the end (`auto output_type = context.get_output_type(); ... if (res.get_element_type() != output_type) { res = std::make_shared<ov::op::v0::Convert>(res, output_type); }`) — the in-code law comment, verbatim: '// Compute in f32: alpha_const/one are f32, and Swish/Add need matching input types.' DECISION CONTENT: (a) at a foreign-graph boundary the SUBGRAPH dtype is negotiable, the CONSTANTS' dtype is not — upcast the inputs, never retag the constants (retagging narrows the constants' precision domain); (b) convert-back-at-the-end preserves the caller's output contract (the graph's edges keep their declared types; only the interior widens); (c) the pair of Convert guards is CONDITIONAL (`!= f32` / `!= output_type`) so an f32 graph pays NOTHING — the upcast exists only at the mismatch. THE HONEST-REFUSAL ARM (the same commit, CUDA side): a backend without the kernel DECLINES the op instead of translating a graph that computes wrong: `if (op->src[0]->type == GGML_TYPE_F16 && ggml_get_glu_op(op) == GGML_GLU_OP_SWIGLU_OAI) { return false; }` in supports_op — F16 SWIGLU_OAI falls to the CPU reference until the CUDA kernel exists. THE REFERENCE ARM (the CPU half): the f16 reference upcasts per element (`const float x = std::min(GGML_CPU_FP16_TO_FP32(src0_p[k]), limit);`), computes the transcendentals in f32 (`out_glu = x / (1.f + expf(alpha * (-x)))`), converts back (`dst_p[k] = GGML_CPU_FP32_TO_FP16(out_glu * (y + 1.f));`) — and its test carries a TOLERANCE THAT NAMES ITS REASON, verbatim: '// F16 epsilon ~ 9.7e-4: relax tolerance vs the F32 default to absorb the natural roundoff drift from the exp()/clamp() chain computed in F16.' (5e-6 for f16 vs 1e-7 for f32 — the reference computes f32 internally; downstream f16 backends get a tolerance that prices THEIR drift honestly). REGIME: any translation/bridge layer that builds typed graphs for a foreign runtime (our FFI/validator graph builders, WGSL pipeline construction over typed buffers, the laya ANE graph converter) — the constants' dtype contract decides the interior dtype; upcast at the edge. ANTI-REGIME: a graph whose constants EXIST in the narrow dtype (nothing pins f32) — upcasting there is pure overhead; and a boundary that already carries explicit dtype-negotiation ops — the mismatch should be fixed at the negotiation, not papered with Converts. KINSHIP (do not double-count): the popcorn upcast trio own NUMERIC-DOMAIN triggers — upcast-before-reduction (the accumulator domain), upcast-before-loss (where precision is lost), batched-upcast-map (batching the conversion) — THIS owns the TRANSLATION-BOUNDARY trigger (a foreign runtime's constant-dtype contract), a graph-construction law, not an accumulation law; dot-macro-family-widens-at-load-f32-accumulate (entries_gpu_59) is the CPU macro-family widening at LOAD for dot products — the N-category arithmetic twin, different trigger (load width, not graph boundary); degrade-not-fail-load-qualification-probe (rust_perf B219) owns the load-time two-class admission — the supports_op refusal here is its op-admission echo at the graph boundary (decline loudly, serve elsewhere); exact-bf16-domain-activation-lut (B189) avoids the transcendentals altogether via exhaustive tables — an alternative to the f32-interior pattern when the domain is enumerable. NotBenchable here (a correctness/reference pair; no perf number ships). Source: ggml-org/llama.cpp @ 5a638aedb7c4352376d1e565a9a420b85f604628 (#29200, MIT) — the commit message (the type-mismatch quote + the precedent-citing sentence), ggml-openvino/openvino/op/glu_swiglu.cpp (the in-code law comment, the input Convert pair, the convert-back), ggml-cuda.cu (the supports_op refusal), ggml-cpu/ops.cpp (the f16 reference upcast loop), tests/test-backend-ops.cpp (the tolerance comment) — every quote verbatim at the fetched .patch this session. Batch 240 / Plan 242 / Research 271.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// naive: the foreign graph's constants are f32 and its typed ops\n// demand matching inputs -- the f16 activation meets them mid-graph.\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq)]\nenum Elem {\n    F16,\n    F32,\n}\n\nstruct Node {\n    elem: Elem,\n}\n\nfn constant_f32(_v: f32) -> Node {\n    Node { elem: Elem::F32 } // created f32 UNCONDITIONALLY\n}\n\nfn translate_naive(src0: &Node, src1: &Node, alpha: f32) -> Node {\n    let gate = clamp(src0, f32::NEG_INFINITY, 2.0);\n    let alpha_const = constant_f32(alpha);\n    let out_glu = swish(gate, alpha_const); // TYPE MISMATCH: f16 and f32\n    let one = constant_f32(1.0);\n    let up = clamp(src1, -2.0, 2.0);\n    out_glu.mul(up.add(one)) // every typed op downstream is mismatched\n}\n\nfn clamp(n: &Node, _lo: f32, _hi: f32) -> Node {\n    n.clone()\n}\n\n fn swish(g: Node, _a: Node) -> Node {\n    g\n}\n\nimpl Node {\n    fn mul(self, _o: Node) -> Node {\n        self\n    }\n    fn add(self, _o: Node) -> Node {\n        self\n    }\n}".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// optimized: upcast every non-f32 INPUT at the boundary, build the\n// subgraph in f32, convert the result back to the caller's type at\n// the end; a backend without the kernel declines the op instead.\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq)]\nenum Elem {\n    F16,\n    F32,\n}\n\nstruct Node {\n    elem: Elem,\n}\n\nfn constant_f32(_v: f32) -> Node {\n    Node { elem: Elem::F32 }\n}\n\nfn convert(_n: &Node, to: Elem) -> Node {\n    Node { elem: to }\n}\n\nfn translate_upcast(src0: &Node, src1: &Node, alpha: f32, output_type: Elem) -> Node {\n    // Compute in f32: alpha_const/one are f32, and Swish/Add need\n    // matching input types.\n    let s0 = if src0.elem != Elem::F32 { convert(src0, Elem::F32) } else { src0.clone() };\n    let s1 = if src1.elem != Elem::F32 { convert(src1, Elem::F32) } else { src1.clone() };\n    let gate = clamp(&s0, f32::NEG_INFINITY, 2.0);\n    let out_glu = swish(gate, constant_f32(alpha));\n    let up = clamp(&s1, -2.0, 2.0);\n    let mut res = out_glu.mul(up.add(constant_f32(1.0)));\n    if res.elem != output_type {\n        res = convert(&res, output_type); // convert back at the end\n    }\n    res\n}\n\n// the honest-refusal arm: no kernel, no translation\nfn cuda_supports(src0_type: Elem, glu_oai: bool) -> bool {\n    !(src0_type == Elem::F16 && glu_oai) // supports_op returns false\n}\n\nfn clamp(n: &Node, _lo: f32, _hi: f32) -> Node {\n    n.clone()\n}\n\nfn swish(g: Node, _a: Node) -> Node {\n    g\n}\n\nimpl Node {\n    fn mul(self, _o: Node) -> Node {\n        self\n    }\n    fn add(self, _o: Node) -> Node {\n        self\n    }\n}".to_string(),
        },
    ]
}
